WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.760
Hi, my name is Riva
and I'm a linguist

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.070
who uses R. Seven years ago, I
tried to learn R and I failed.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.130
I wasn't sure where to start
my R journey so as someone

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.190
with a background in the humanities,
I thought the best option was

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.410
reading a book.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.030
I wanted to make one
of those cool plots

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.680
with a great background
that I've seen around

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.580
to communicate some results.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.240
So I picked a book that promised
me that I will learn the most

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.460
important elements of R.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.450
On page 99, I was still
reading about defining

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.750
classes and methods,

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.640
trying to understand
what an array was

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.190
and how to multiply its
elements but hadn't done

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.580
anything with real data yet.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.680
The problem I think
were my expectations.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.480
I was expecting
learning resources

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.615
that help me say
things with data

00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:51.990
but all I found
were description

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.950
of rules that didn't make much
sense to me as a beginner.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.720
It was very difficult
for me to see

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.840
how I can use these rules
with the data I have

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.780
and how I will jump from
these abstract descriptions

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.750
to, for example, making a plot.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.630
It felt like trying to learn a
second language only by reading

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.610
a grammar book and
not by interacting

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.540
with actual human beings.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.610
The thing is that learning
a programming language

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.580
has many similarities with
learning a second language.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.930
But what does this mean?

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.410
How can this idea be applied
to the design of learning

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:28.160
resources or when choosing them
to guide your learning path.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.110
And how can you take
this into account

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.050
when developing a
package for example.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:37.040
First, let's talk
about learning.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.750
Not everyone learns
a second language

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.730
with the same purposes.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.220
The same happen with
programming languages.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.550
I fail learning R the first
time because I wasn't the expected

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.860
audience for the book I chose.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.350
At the moment, I just
wanted to be another user

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.030
not a programmer.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.030
Not everyone is learning R to
be a linguist or a grammarian

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.200
of the language.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.540
There are surely people that
want a deep understanding

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.235
of how the language works.

00:02:01.235 --> 00:02:03.110
And those are the people
who are contributing

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.790
to its development.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.530
But there are tons
of people that

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:10.100
just want to use R to do
things like making a plot,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.520
or running statistical
model, or creating reports.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.860
I failed the first time
I tried to learn R

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.480
because the book I
chose for learning

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.090
was the equivalent of
a grammar book of R,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.980
descriptions of how
the language works

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.350
and the names and purposes
of all its different parts.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.430
And as a beginner or someone who
just want to learn the language

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.260
to communicate
your data to others

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.480
that is not what is more useful.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.320
What you need is something that
works more like a textbook.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.070
Textbooks for learning a second
language always start the same.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.830
You learn how to say Hello,
how to introduce yourself,

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.400
and some simple sentences to
communicate meaning to others.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.880
Even books for learning dead
languages have this approach.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.590
I had to learn a
little bit of Latin

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.750
as an undergrad
and the first thing

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.110
we learned was how to ask
someone, who they are,

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.780
and how to respond
to that question.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.020
I even learned how to
insult someone in Latin.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.260
A language in which the last
native speaker

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.750
died centuries ago.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.910
Textbooks start this
way because they

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.060
are built around people needs for
knowledge not our own language

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.110
rules.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.450
In textbooks, you
learn first what

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:21.710
you can do with the language
not how the language works.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.430
In language teaching, this is
called a communicative approach

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.740
and it's based on the idea
that learning a language

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.230
successfully comes through
having to communicate

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.660
real meaning to real people.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.790
Throughout the different
lessons in a textbook,

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.940
you learn new words and
structures and new context

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:41.030
where to use those
words and structures.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.700
The grammar rules behind
those real life examples

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.010
are only explained after
you learn how to use them.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.500
And if and only if it's really
necessary for you to know them.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.950
In textbooks, scaffolding
work as a spiral.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.020
You learn something
in one lesson

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.000
and then come back to that
content in the next lesson.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.020
By now showing more ways
to use what you learn,

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.060
how to adapt it to
different situations,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.935
and how to combine the
words and structures

00:04:06.935 --> 00:04:10.820
you know, in your ways.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.180
It's like copying and
pasting and then adapting.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.300
You learn how to use a new
language structure like,

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.279
how to ask a question.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.580
And then you learn how to
adapt it to your own needs,

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.740
to solve the problems
you're interested in.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.400
Its this ability to adapt
the language structures

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.870
to new situations,

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.440
that makes us flexible users
in both natural languages

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.600
and programming ones.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.290
For example, we first learned
how to create one type of plot

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.360
using all the default settings.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.130
And then we can
back to that content

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.800
to add more complexity
to the association

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.920
like mapping more variables,
using different geoms,

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.020
or making annotations.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.150
And we build those new
skills over the ones

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.100
we learned previously.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.660
This copy, pasting,
and adapting is also

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.480
the strategy we
use with examples

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.380
we find in Stack Overflow
or RStudio Community.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.850
We bring to our
scripts something

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.230
that work in another
context and then try

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:11.000
to figure out how to
adapt it to our own needs.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.770
Sometimes we're
not even sure why

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.440
it didn't worked but after
doing it a couple of times

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.470
we start inferring what is
going on behind the scenes.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.600
You don't need to know
all the grammar rules

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.120
to be understood in a language.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.670
You don't need to know the
name of all its structures

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.980
to be a competent users.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.760
Even native speakers don't
know the name of all the parts.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.340
You probably don't
know what a paratactic

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:39.090
or a hypotactic clause
complex is but you definitely

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.040
know how to use one.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.900
If you're a native
English speaker,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.520
you know how to
correctly pronounce

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.630
every case of two O's together
in words like moon, book,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.070
floor, and flood.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.890
Something very, very difficult
for us not native speakers.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:57.840
But you probably don't know why
they're pronounced differently.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.780
The adults who raised you didn't
read your grammar book for you

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.850
to acquire your native language.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.150
They didn't teach you
the grammar rules.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.710
They talked to you
and let you talk.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.620
They probably read you books.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.750
You sung or sang together.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.500
You did things with words
progressively more complex.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.300
In the same way, you
don't need to know

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.040
all the rules of a
programming language

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.040
to be able to do things with it.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.750
What rules do you need?

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.030
The ones that can help you
achieve the purposes you're

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.650
seeking.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.440
If you need to customize a
plot in a very precise way

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.260
because the default options
are not what you need,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.360
then learning how a
thing works in ggplot

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.180
is relevant; not before.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.750
If you need to work with array
then learning what they are

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.850
and what kind of things
you can do with them

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.960
is relevant; not before.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.630
One more thing about
textbooks, they usually

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.640
have learned our personas.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.490
If you're learning a language
because you want to travel,

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.740
the first chapters
will probably show you

00:06:57.740 --> 00:06:59.900
how to get along in
the airport and ask

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.060
for directions in a city.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.070
If you're learning the
language because you're

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.680
going to study abroad,
the first chapters

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.480
will probably be
about students trying

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.130
to find something
in the campus

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.070
or dealing with
University bureaucracy.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:16.970
The same happens with
programming learning resources.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.040
Fortunately in the
past couple of years,

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.320
a lot of online resources
have appeared to learn R

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.120
in other programming languages.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.390
And many of them are focused
on the needs of specific users.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:28.490
So all the data and
examples are connected

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.000
with what those specific
users need to know.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.550
So taking into account
the communicative approach

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:35.990
when teaching
programming language

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:40.460
involves: Doing real things
from day or page one.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:44.450
Building complexity as a spiral,
revealing rules after you know

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.930
how and when to use them, and
all the ones that can help you

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.600
achieve what you're looking for.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.010
And take into account who
your learner personas are.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:57.800
In the last couple of
years a lot of R resources

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.220
have been created taking into
account these principles.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.990
Learning resources that
let you experiment first

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.230
and then build the explanations.

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.180
Books that show
you the whole game

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.140
first and then explain the
details behind every step.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.900
Publication that had in mind
different learning personas

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.300
with different
backgrounds that show you

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:22.390
how to do relevant things with
relevant data for your field.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.160
But hey, what about
the other books?

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.440
The ones that
explain how R works.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.020
Well, those actually
a very cool books.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.169
I'm a linguist myself so I
really love grammar books.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:34.270
But for both natural and
programming languages

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:36.490
they're not the first step
to approach a language

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.650
when what you want is
to learn how to use it

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.159
and how to communicate
with other people.

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:44.080
But is this
communicative approach

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.320
only relevant for people
teaching or learning R?

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:48.910
What about programmers?

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.920
What about people
developing packages?

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.470
Well, thinking
back at development

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:55.000
from a communicative
point of view

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.000
can also help us in issues
like developing more error

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.500
messages and documentation.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.220
Let's take a look at
error messages first.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.170
Whatever you are
learning, having feedback

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.290
is the kind of thing that
can help you improve.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:10.690
This applies to
any human activity

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.710
from sports to cooking, from
meeting to language learning.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:16.660
Having this in mind,
let's take a look

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.620
at two error messages
addressing the same problem.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:23.890
We all have been here putting
one instead of two equal signs

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:26.080
to filter a column.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.020
This is the error message
you get in base R.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:32.800
It says, error unexpected
equal sign in and then the part

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.720
of the code where the
unexpected equal sign is.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.490
Error message here
is pointing you

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:44.290
where your mistake is but there
is no clue on how to solve it.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.480
Why is the equal
sign unexpected,

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.808
should I put something else?

00:09:48.808 --> 00:09:50.350
And if you're not
an English speaker,

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.320
there is even more enigmatic.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.890
Pointing errors is not the
same as giving feedback.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.110
To learn from your
mistakes, you not only

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.900
need to know where the
error is but you need

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.860
some clues on how to solve it.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.600
So next time you
know what to do.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.310
Let's take a look at the
message for the same error

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:12.580
but using the filter
function from dplyr.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:17.110
The error message says, error
problem with filter input 1,

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.030
Input 1 is named.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.680
This usually means
that you have used

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.140
one equal sign instead of two.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:26.470
Did you mean country two
equal signs is Chile?

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:30.310
Run rlang::last_error() to see
where the error occurred.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:34.090
This message let us know
what the problem is,

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:36.790
its probable origin,
a suggestion how

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:39.550
it can be solved,
and if and only

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.440
if you want to know
where your code fail,

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:44.570
how to take a detailed look.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:47.030
What we have here is feedback.

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.610
We not only learned the
origin of the problem

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.770
and how to solve
it, but also how

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.550
we can take a deeper
look at the code

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:56.100
to see where the error occured.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.650
And if you're not
an English speaker

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.360
you can put this
on Google Translate

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.030
and we'll get something useful.

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:04.430
These couple of extra lines
in your function code

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:09.590
can make the life of
many people easier.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.770
And help them feel in
a way they can learn.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:14.840
Giving useful feedback
is the best way

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.060
to learn how to
overcome difficulties.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.420
And this is something we do
all the time when someone is

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:22.650
learning our native language.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:25.430
If you don't understand what
they are trying to communicate,

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:29.240
you usually say things
like, sorry, did you mean--

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.090
and try to rephrase what they
said to check if you understand

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.890
correctly their intention.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:37.550
We don't go around saying people
lack of subject-verb agreement

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.220
or misplaced modifiers.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.200
We don't do that
because it is not

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.390
really helpful for learners.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:48.560
It's more effective to give
cues on how to fix the problem

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.840
and show how accurate
version might look like.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:53.960
So if you're a package
developer and want people

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.090
to be happy users
with your package,

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:57.590
you can think about
how your error

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.380
messages can give guidance.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.240
But error messages
are not the only part

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:06.120
of a package that can adopt
a communicative approach.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.960
Let's take a look
at documentation.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.270
Function documentation is great.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.280
Sometimes you forget
the name of an argument

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.170
and taking the whole
page can allow you

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.390
to solve this kind of issue.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.520
But do you think you need
to know the actual name

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.410
of the function you're after?

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.210
Otherwise it's kind of useless.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.990
What if you know the
problem you want to solve

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:31.100
but not the name of the function
that can help you solve it?

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:34.400
Function documentation
are like dictionaries.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.020
They have the
definition of the words

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.480
you can use in a
programming language

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.210
but we don't learn a
language by memorizing words

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.740
from the dictionary.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:45.710
We need to learn how to use
the words, in what context

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:48.230
they are useful,
what kind of meaning

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.630
can we construct with them.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:53.720
What if you don't know the
name of the function you need?

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.830
What if you know what
you want to achieve

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.920
but not what functions can
be useful for that.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.370
Well, here comes vignette.

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.500
Vignettes are awesome resources

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.480
to describe the problem
that your package is

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:09.140
designed to solve and to
show people how to solve it.

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:10.850
They are a great
resource to show

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:15.140
how to do things with your
package with real example.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.630
And they are easy to find
using a search engine

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:21.300
so they are also a great
way to promote your package.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.140
In a vignette, you can
explain with details

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:27.200
the different categories
your functions are divided.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:31.250
How you can coordinate multiple
functions to solve problems.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.830
And show extended examples
that people can copy,

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:37.050
paste, and adapt to
meet their own needs.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:38.870
The best part is
that vignettes are

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.930
easy to create using
the usethis package.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:46.110
With usethis::use_vignette(),
you can create one.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:48.350
So thinking of error
messages as a way

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:51.050
to give feedback to the
users of your package

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:54.080
and writing vignettes that
explain how to do things

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.420
with functions you
create, are great ways

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.340
to think about
package development

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.270
from a communicative approach.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:05.310
Two closing remarks: is really
R the best programming language

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:06.850
to learn?

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:10.120
Isn't Python better
or what about Julia?

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.670
With programming language
happened the same as

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.480
with natural languages.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:17.990
There is no one intrinsically
better than the other.

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:19.720
The best programming
language is the one

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.150
that can help you solve more
effectively the problem you

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:25.820
are trying to solve in the
context you currently are.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.940
Sometimes things
like processing time

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:32.580
or a number of lines of code are
important to take into account

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.700
but sometimes they're not.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.190
And only what is easier
or faster for you

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:41.660
to write not the computer to
process is more important.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.450
And sometimes it's more relevant
what programming language

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.730
your audience can understand.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:49.520
The same happens with
natural languages.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.430
Right now, I would really
prefer to be speaking in Spanish

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.100
but it's definitely
more effective

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.110
if I speak in English.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:58.010
Why?

00:14:58.010 --> 00:14:59.480
Not because of
any characteristic

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.490
of English language itself.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.620
Actually for this
subject in particular,

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:07.220
Spanish is way more precise
because we have more words

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:08.660
to speak about language.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.760
We make distinctions
like lengua,

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.190
lenguaje, and idioma
all of which have

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:15.200
different and precise meanings.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.000
So I need to use
more words in English

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.040
to explain those concepts.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:22.250
But right now, being able to
convey one message to a broader

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:26.000
audience is more important than
lexical variety and precision

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.580
that Spanish offers
to the subject.

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.100
So which programming
language to lean?

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.320
The one that can help you
best solve the problems

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.810
you're interested
in and that allow

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.310
you to speak with
the people you're

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:40.760
interested in communicating
with at that moment.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:47.030
And the same way as
with natural languages,

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:50.270
knowing more than one allow you
to communicate with more people

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.640
and do more things with words.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:57.080
And this leads us to the
second closing remark.

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:01.490
Communication and community
has the same etymology.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:04.100
A communicative approach
in programming languages

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:08.210
is important because it can help
us build stronger communities

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:11.060
where everyone can be
part of the conversation.

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:12.950
New people learning
the language can

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.750
offer new and
diverse perspectives

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:17.120
and we should cherish that.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:20.540
There is no one correct way to
learn a programming language

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:22.010
but there are many
things that we

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:27.230
can be done to make it more
effective and enjoyable.

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:29.580
Failing to offer people
opportunities to learn

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.540
in an easier way or
thinking that the only way

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:35.180
to learn a programming
language is the hard way

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:38.120
is not only kind of
a gatekeeping issue

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:39.710
but it's also dangerous.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:41.330
Because the most
dangerous thing that

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:43.010
can happen to our
natural programming

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.830
language is not having a
vibrant community of users

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.670
to keep it alive and evolving.

